QScintilla is a port to Qt of Neil Hodgson's Scintilla C++ editor
control released by Riverbank Computing.

As well as features found in standard text editing components,
QScintilla includes features especially useful when editing and
debugging source code. These include support for syntax styling, error
indicators, code completion and call tips. The selection margin can
contain markers like those used in debuggers to indicate breakpoints
and the current line. Styling choices are more open than with many
editors, allowing the use of proportional fonts, bold and italics,
multiple foreground and background colours and multiple fonts.

The Slackware package QScintilla is available in Slackware 15.0 Stable
but it has been removed from Slackware -current on June 2026 because it
is not more needed by any package released by official Slackware.
The purpose of this slackbuild is to make QScintilla available in
-current as well, for use it to build and installing software that is
not released by official Slackware but requires QScintilla as
dependency, such as QGIS.

Warning
Make sure that install the package PyQt-builder before build
QScintilla: unlike previous installations in, -current PyQt-builder is
needed to build and install the Python bindings for full functionality
of QScintilla (required by QGIS, for example). Without this dependency,
the build of Python bindings will fail and the script will stop. You
can find PyQt-builder in the same repository of QScintilla for -current
users. 

Both QScintilla and PyQt-builder packages are not available in the
repository of Slackbuilds.org because they are designed for Slackware
-current users. QScintilla is already available in official Slackware
15.0 Stable and PyQt-builder is not compatible with Stable version.
